/F2 17.00 Tf
1 0 0 1 60.00 571.00 Tm
(Chapter 10: Storing Your Harvest) Tj
0.720 0.470 0.100 rg
/F2 11.00 Tf
1 0 0 1 74.00 536.50 Tm
(More Than They Could Eat) Tj
0.120 0.120 0.120 rg
/F1 10.50 Tf
1 0 0 1 74.00 521.00 Tm
(Grace's single tomato plant did too well - she had more than she and her partner could possibly) Tj
/F1 10.50 Tf
1 0 0 1 74.00 505.50 Tm
(eat, and beautiful fruit was starting to soften on the counter. Rather than let it rot, she did two) Tj
/F1 10.50 Tf
1 0 0 1 74.00 490.00 Tm
(things: she dried a batch in the oven on its lowest setting for winter sauces, and she carried a) Tj
/F1 10.50 Tf
1 0 0 1 74.00 474.50 Tm
(paper bag of the rest down the hall to her neighbors. They were delighted - and a week later) Tj
/F1 10.50 Tf
1 0 0 1 74.00 459.00 Tm
(returned the bag full of their own surplus peppers. Grace realized her little balcony hadn't just fed) Tj
/F1 10.50 Tf
1 0 0 1 74.00 443.50 Tm
(her; it had quietly turned strangers into friends. Nothing went to waste, and everyone ate better.) Tj
0.000 0.000 0.000 rg
/F1 11.00 Tf
1 0 0 1 60.00 413.00 Tm
(A thriving garden often gives you more than you can eat at once - a lovely problem with easy) Tj
/F1 11.00 Tf
1 0 0 1 60.00 396.50 Tm
(solutions. Don't let a good harvest go to waste. Here's how to keep it.) Tj
/F1 11.00 Tf
1 0 0 1 60.00 374.00 Tm
(General methods:) Tj
/F1 11.00 Tf
1 0 0 1 76.00 352.50 Tm
(- Refrigerate for short-term freshness \(many veg keep days to a week\).) Tj
/F1 11.00 Tf
1 0 0 1 76.00 337.00 Tm
(- Cool, dark, dry storage \(a pantry, cupboard, or "root cellar" corner\) for keepers like onions,) Tj
/F1 11.00 Tf
1 0 0 1 76.00 321.50 Tm
(    potatoes, and other roots.) Tj
/F1 11.00 Tf
1 0 0 1 76.00 306.00 Tm
(- Drying - a food dehydrator, or an oven on its lowest setting \(~140∞F\), watching so veg dry rather) Tj
/F1 11.00 Tf
1 0 0 1 76.00 290.50 Tm
(    than roast. Great for peppers, tomatoes, beans.) Tj
/F1 11.00 Tf
1 0 0 1 76.00 275.00 Tm
(- Canning / preserving - mason jars and simple preserving turn a glut into winter treats.) Tj
/F1 11.00 Tf
1 0 0 1 76.00 259.50 Tm
(- Freezing - excellent for corn \(husk removed\), beans, and more.) Tj
/F1 11.00 Tf
1 0 0 1 76.00 244.00 Tm
(- Share the surplus - neighbors, friends, and family love fresh organic veg, and generosity has a) Tj
/F1 11.00 Tf
1 0 0 1 76.00 228.50 Tm
(    way of coming back around.) Tj
/F1 11.00 Tf
1 0 0 1 60.00 206.00 Tm
(Crop-by-crop quick tips:) Tj
/F1 11.00 Tf
1 0 0 1 76.00 184.50 Tm
(- Lettuce - wash, dry, bag, and keep in the fridge crisper \(~a week\). Start eating it even before full) Tj
/F1 11.00 Tf
1 0 0 1 76.00 169.00 Tm
(    size; share the rest.) Tj
/F1 11.00 Tf
1 0 0 1 76.00 153.50 Tm
(- Root vegetables \(potatoes, carrots, turnips\) - store undamaged ones in a cool, dark, dry place;) Tj
/F1 11.00 Tf
1 0 0 1 76.00 138.00 Tm
(    discard any bruised ones \(spoilage spreads\). Carrots also dry well; turnips can sit in the fridge) Tj
/F1 11.00 Tf
1 0 0 1 76.00 122.50 Tm
(    crisper.) Tj
/F1 11.00 Tf
1 0 0 1 76.00 107.00 Tm
(- Tomatoes - keep slightly-green ones on the counter/windowsill; ripe ones in the fridge; dry or) Tj
/F1 11.00 Tf
1 0 0 1 76.00 91.50 Tm
(    preserve extras for sauces.) Tj
/F1 11.00 Tf
1 0 0 1 76.00 76.00 Tm
(- Onions - store in any dry, dark place; keep only unbruised, moisture-free bulbs.) Tj
/F1 11.00 Tf
1 0 0 1 76.00 60.50 Tm
(- Beans & peas - fridge for a few days, can with proper equipment, or dry beans for soaking and) Tj
ET
endstream
endobj
